WEBVTT

00:32.206 --> 00:35.078
The 1990s were a golden era

00:35.122 --> 00:36.558
for The Walt Disney Company.

00:36.558 --> 00:37.907
The Little Mermaid 
revived the company

00:37.907 --> 00:39.996
from the edge of bankruptcy.

00:39.996 --> 00:43.086
Beauty and the Beast earned
a Best Picture nomination.

00:43.130 --> 00:46.785
And The Lion King broke
box office records worldwide.

00:48.657 --> 00:53.357
But the reality just miles away
was no fairy tale.

00:53.357 --> 00:57.796
In 1992, a state jury acquitted

00:57.883 --> 01:01.670
four LAPD officers
on almost all charges

01:01.713 --> 01:03.628
in the beating
of Rodney G. King.

01:05.674 --> 01:09.373
The response was anger.

01:09.460 --> 01:13.029
Fire, chaos,

01:13.029 --> 01:16.337
massive citywide destruction.

01:16.380 --> 01:20.254
It was a pivotal moment
for Black culture in the '90s.

01:20.341 --> 01:23.953
But it's a little-known fact
that another cultural milestone

01:24.040 --> 01:26.390
emerged from that fateful day.

01:28.479 --> 01:31.265
It's a story so unbelievable,

01:31.352 --> 01:33.745
it could only happen
in Hollywood.

01:35.051 --> 01:38.837
How did this young,
Black, unassuming animator

01:38.837 --> 01:42.841
from East Atlanta wind up

01:42.841 --> 01:45.627
as the CEO of Disney

01:45.627 --> 01:49.544
at the company's most powerful
moment in time?

02:12.132 --> 02:15.222
- Okay, rolling.
- Speed.

02:15.309 --> 02:17.093
Believe it or not,

02:17.180 --> 02:18.964
we named him after Tom Jones.

02:19.008 --> 02:23.230
♪

02:23.230 --> 02:25.188
Yeah!

02:25.232 --> 02:28.626
His father
used to play Tom Jones.

02:28.670 --> 02:31.673
That's how he first courted me.

02:31.673 --> 02:35.285
He would do a little dance
and we would laugh.

02:35.329 --> 02:37.418
♪ I got to tell you a story♪

02:37.461 --> 02:39.420
♪ Every man ought to know...♪

02:39.463 --> 02:41.248
Usually, the boys
in Ronald's family

02:41.248 --> 02:44.120
was named after their fathers,

02:44.207 --> 02:46.905
but when we had our boy,
we knew that

02:46.992 --> 02:48.429
he was gonna be different,

02:48.472 --> 02:50.474
so we had to do something different.

02:50.474 --> 02:54.261
We had to name him Thomas.

02:54.348 --> 02:57.873
T loved cartoons
something crazy.

02:57.916 --> 02:59.788
I mean, he was always drawing
on his desk, uh,

02:59.875 --> 03:02.878
in, uh, in the margins of
his notebook, in the textbooks,

03:02.921 --> 03:05.228
all on the walls, man.

03:05.272 --> 03:06.925
He just drove
our teachers crazy.

03:07.012 --> 03:11.713
He was obsessed with this
one cat named, um, Blasto Boy?

03:11.800 --> 03:14.281
No, no, no, Astro Boy.

03:19.503 --> 03:21.940
Yeah, yeah, I used to
watch it with him,

03:21.940 --> 03:23.159
and I remember thinking...

03:23.203 --> 03:26.945
this is kind of boring.

03:26.945 --> 03:28.295
But Thomas loved it, man.

03:28.382 --> 03:30.514
He drew... he drew
that dude everywhere.

03:30.601 --> 03:33.735
One time, he asked me, he said,

03:33.735 --> 03:37.173
"What if Astro Boy
came here and saved us?"

03:37.173 --> 03:39.610
I said, "Saved us from what?"

03:39.697 --> 03:44.572
And he just kind of shrugged
and said, "Everything."

03:48.967 --> 03:50.926
I mean, what kind
of eight-year-old

03:50.969 --> 03:53.189
is thinking like that,
you know?

03:53.189 --> 03:54.538
I mean, I think he was scared.

03:54.625 --> 03:56.845
He wasn't no punk,
I'm just saying, I don't

03:56.845 --> 03:59.761
think he ever understood
why the world was so unfair.

03:59.761 --> 04:01.545
And where we were growing up...

04:01.632 --> 04:04.200
it was, like, 
unfair unfair, you know?

04:04.200 --> 04:06.289
I mean, you could be
sitting at home watching

04:06.333 --> 04:08.552
a program with white folks
with nice houses.

04:08.552 --> 04:09.771
We couldn't even
ride ours bikes outside,

04:09.771 --> 04:11.207
cause niggas
will take it from you.

04:11.294 --> 04:14.645
Straight to your face,
they do not care. Crime?

04:14.645 --> 04:16.343
What, so? They will
ride your bike in front

04:16.430 --> 04:17.474
of your house the next day.

04:19.433 --> 04:21.565
Yeah, unfair.

04:21.565 --> 04:23.437
The boys picked on him.

04:23.524 --> 04:26.570
He would come home
sometimes with pages

04:26.657 --> 04:28.355
torn out of
his little sketchbook.

04:28.355 --> 04:30.444
Yeah, we would call him white.

04:30.487 --> 04:31.880
We was just messing with him.

04:31.923 --> 04:33.316
Um, I think he was
just trying to fit in,

04:33.360 --> 04:35.753
but he wasn't really like us,
you know?

04:35.797 --> 04:37.233
And the kids
would call him on it.

04:37.320 --> 04:40.236
I mean, he just wanted to draw,

04:40.236 --> 04:44.022
and he didn't understand why
that wasn't "cool," you know?

04:44.109 --> 04:46.677
I mean, niggas will
make fun of you for anything,

04:46.677 --> 04:49.245
and I don't think
Thomas was built to take it.

04:49.332 --> 04:51.595
I told him, "Son,

04:51.595 --> 04:56.513
one day, ain't none of this
gonna even matter."

05:12.007 --> 05:15.706
In 1987, Thomas left
East Atlanta to attend

05:15.750 --> 05:19.101
Savannah College
of Art and Design, or SCAD.

05:20.276 --> 05:22.234
It was here that Thomas's

05:22.278 --> 05:25.673
creative dreams
began to take shape.

05:25.716 --> 05:28.850
We didn't have a lot of Black
students in the program then.

05:28.937 --> 05:32.157
Thomas showed a lot of promise.

05:32.201 --> 05:35.944
In his application,
he wrote about how he wanted

05:35.987 --> 05:38.425
to work at Disney one day.

05:38.425 --> 05:40.514
We usually laugh
at that sort of thing.

05:40.557 --> 05:42.733
Everybody says that, you know?

05:42.820 --> 05:47.999
But with his application,
he sent a little flip-book.

05:47.999 --> 05:49.479
Can you see this?

05:53.091 --> 05:56.530
I mean, he had the sensibility

05:56.530 --> 06:00.795
and the wit
of a true Disney animator.

06:03.058 --> 06:07.497
Art Babbitt was a big name
at Disney Animation for a time.

06:07.541 --> 06:09.456
As an animator
in the '30s and '40s,

06:09.543 --> 06:12.110
he racked up a lot of credits,

06:12.154 --> 06:13.677
and enemies,

06:13.764 --> 06:15.897
but he was most well-known for

06:15.984 --> 06:18.247
developing
the character of Goofy.

06:18.247 --> 06:21.946
He came to SCAD, and he spoke.

06:36.918 --> 06:39.311
Thomas really gravitated to him.

06:39.355 --> 06:42.576
He became obsessed,
and he really glommed

06:42.663 --> 06:46.362
on to Babbitt's article
"Analysis of the Goof."

06:48.146 --> 06:50.105
Art describes Goofy.

06:50.148 --> 06:52.368
He says, "Think of
the Goof as a composite

06:52.368 --> 06:56.372
"of an everlasting optimist,
a gullible Good Samaritan,

06:56.459 --> 07:00.158
"a half-wit, a shiftless,

07:00.158 --> 07:01.290
"good-natured colored boy,

07:01.377 --> 07:02.683
"and a hick.

07:02.726 --> 07:04.467
"He is loose-jointed

07:04.511 --> 07:06.904
"and gangly, but not rubbery.

07:06.948 --> 07:09.516
"He can move fast if he has to,
but would rather

07:09.603 --> 07:12.388
"avoid overexertion,

07:12.475 --> 07:15.391
"so he takes what seems
the easiest way.

07:15.478 --> 07:19.743
"He is a philosopher of
the barber shop variety.

07:19.830 --> 07:23.051
"No matter what happens,
he accepts it finally

07:23.094 --> 07:27.621
as being for the best
or at least amusing."

07:27.708 --> 07:32.626
Thomas used this text
as the center of a piece

07:32.669 --> 07:36.456
of a series he called
"Goofy, Please."

07:37.979 --> 07:41.417
After class one day, he
showed me some of these pieces,

07:41.504 --> 07:44.202
and I looked at it,
and I looked at him,

07:44.246 --> 07:45.987
and I thought,

07:46.074 --> 07:49.556
"This kid is from
another universe,

07:49.556 --> 07:52.559
like, a whole nother planet."

07:52.646 --> 07:55.300
Thomas would be like,
"Hey, come on,

07:55.344 --> 07:58.434
you know Goofy's a [bleep]."

07:58.434 --> 08:00.523
:
And we would just laugh, man.

08:00.567 --> 08:02.960
We just thought
that was so funny.

08:03.004 --> 08:06.790
It was clear that
Thomas was very talented.

08:06.877 --> 08:08.400
For his thesis,

08:08.444 --> 08:11.360
he made this
incredible short film.

08:12.100 --> 08:15.320
♪ Well, I'd like to visit
the moon♪

08:15.364 --> 08:19.455
♪ On a rocket ship
high in the air...♪

08:19.499 --> 08:21.805
He had little screenings;

08:21.892 --> 08:24.329
teachers would come,
and students.

08:24.373 --> 08:28.246
The film felt so deep.

08:28.333 --> 08:30.684
I-I think it was somehow
connected to him

08:30.684 --> 08:32.860
losing his father
in freshman year.

08:32.903 --> 08:34.557
♪ I would miss
all the places and...♪

08:34.601 --> 08:36.124
Uncle Ronnie dying
changed him, man.

08:36.167 --> 08:39.040
Thomas was in his
freshman year at SCAD,

08:39.127 --> 08:41.042
and I didn't think he
was going to make it, but, uh,

08:41.129 --> 08:43.479
once we buried
my Uncle Ronnie, man,

08:43.566 --> 08:47.527
he, uh-- it's like
his brain exploded.

08:49.267 --> 08:53.054
Depending on who saw it
and when they saw it,

08:53.054 --> 08:56.274
it was either
the funniest thing,

08:56.361 --> 08:58.625
or the saddest thing
you'd ever seen.

08:58.712 --> 09:02.846
That short was what
got him into Disney.

09:02.846 --> 09:04.195
No question.

09:08.722 --> 09:11.725
Washington comes to Disney

09:11.812 --> 09:14.292
right out of college,
fresh from SCAD.

09:14.379 --> 09:18.166
You know, he's there,
the company's doing pretty well,

09:18.209 --> 09:19.646
they've just had their hit
with The Little Mermaid.

09:21.473 --> 09:24.302
One of the things that-that
I'm glad that I was

09:24.389 --> 09:26.261
able to be part of
was that, um,

09:26.304 --> 09:29.960
Disney instituted this
diversity program.

09:30.047 --> 09:34.530
We knew that we needed to
bring in younger voices

09:34.574 --> 09:37.185
from a diversified
point of view,

09:37.228 --> 09:39.317
and that would actually, uh,

09:39.361 --> 09:41.885
strengthen our company.

09:41.885 --> 09:45.062
And Thomas was one
of the first of those.

09:45.106 --> 09:48.718
Thomas started out at
Disney as an assistant animator

09:48.762 --> 09:52.330
on DuckTales the Movie:
Treasure of the Lost Lamp.

09:52.330 --> 09:55.116
It wasn't necessarily
creative work,

09:55.203 --> 09:57.422
but things are fine, you know.

09:57.466 --> 10:00.512
It's not really moving
as fast as he wants things

10:00.556 --> 10:02.645
to be moving,
but he's doing all right.

10:02.689 --> 10:05.561
And then the uprisings happened.

10:11.654 --> 10:13.264
When the shooting happened,

10:13.351 --> 10:16.311
and then the riots,

10:16.354 --> 10:19.662
Thomas was working in L.A.

10:19.706 --> 10:23.710
He called me
and he said, "Mama,

10:23.710 --> 10:27.278
I just want to go down there."

10:27.278 --> 10:30.891
And I told him,

10:30.934 --> 10:34.590
"Son, you need to riot

10:34.677 --> 10:36.244
with that pen and paper."

10:36.287 --> 10:38.594
He was shook.

10:38.594 --> 10:40.161
I could see it in him,
he was shook.

10:40.248 --> 10:42.380
He actually called me
one time and was like,

10:42.467 --> 10:45.166
"If I ever get the chance
to do a movie around here,

10:45.253 --> 10:47.777
I'm not holding back."

10:59.049 --> 11:01.138
While South Central Los Angeles

11:01.182 --> 11:03.401
was engulfed in flames,

11:03.488 --> 11:07.623
15 miles north in Burbank,
another fire was about to erup.

11:07.710 --> 11:11.453
What happened next is
one of the most unlikely

11:11.496 --> 11:15.718
and unprecedented events in the
history of corporate America.

11:15.762 --> 11:17.938
After the success
of Beauty and the Beast,

11:17.981 --> 11:20.941
Michael Eisner put
Dennis Stephenson in charge

11:20.984 --> 11:23.421
while he went on a sabbatical.

11:23.508 --> 11:26.076
But two weeks into the job,

11:26.120 --> 11:28.513
Stephenson had
a health emergency

11:28.557 --> 11:30.907
and passed shortly thereafter...

11:32.648 --> 11:34.824
...leaving the job
open once more.

11:36.521 --> 11:38.523
We all voted for this guy

11:38.567 --> 11:39.786
named Tom Washington.

11:39.873 --> 11:41.570
But we didn't realize

11:41.657 --> 11:43.746
that Tom's first name

11:43.790 --> 11:48.229
was actually Thompson,
not Thomas.

11:48.316 --> 11:51.667
Thomas Washington
was an animator.

11:51.667 --> 11:54.670
People were pretty upset
when they found out

11:54.670 --> 11:55.802
they voted for the wrong man.

11:58.456 --> 12:00.589
It felt like an honest mistake
that we

12:00.589 --> 12:03.200
could sweep under the rug, but

12:03.244 --> 12:04.593
after Beauty and the Beast,

12:04.680 --> 12:07.117
there were a lot of eyes on us.

12:07.161 --> 12:08.553
We tried to handle it smoothly,

12:08.597 --> 12:11.687
but Thomas held our feet
to the fire.

12:13.558 --> 12:17.040
So, because of this,
you know, "behind closed door,

12:17.040 --> 12:19.564
boys' club" handshake,

12:19.608 --> 12:21.697
Disney is now in radical

12:21.697 --> 12:24.265
new territory
but entirely by default.

12:24.265 --> 12:26.833
So you have this young animato,

12:26.920 --> 12:29.139
this young, Black animator

12:29.139 --> 12:32.490
who comes into this company,
all of a sudden has a ton

12:32.577 --> 12:36.059
of power at a hugely
culturally significant moment,

12:36.146 --> 12:38.061
and he wants
to do something with it.

12:38.148 --> 12:39.280
He wants to prove himself.

12:39.367 --> 12:41.282
This cat was on my line

12:41.369 --> 12:42.936
with some foolishness,

12:42.936 --> 12:44.502
talking about
he's the CEO of Disney.

12:44.502 --> 12:47.418
I was, I was crying laughing.

12:49.507 --> 12:53.511
And then, you know,
he took me to his office

12:53.598 --> 12:55.949
and people was,
like, greeting him.

12:55.949 --> 12:59.343
First day,
he took us all into a room

12:59.387 --> 13:01.302
that we call the stock room,
which is where everybody

13:01.302 --> 13:02.869
hangs out before
we get to work.

13:02.869 --> 13:05.872
And he shows us
a frame of animation

13:05.872 --> 13:10.746
of Goofy in Mickey's trailer,
and, uh,

13:10.746 --> 13:13.096
Mickey has Pluto on a leash.

13:13.183 --> 13:16.099
And it gets real quiet,

13:16.186 --> 13:18.406
and, uh, Washington goes,

13:18.449 --> 13:21.104
"Why is Goofy
letting Mickey do that?"

13:21.191 --> 13:23.280
He's a very fine dog, sir.

13:23.324 --> 13:25.630
And, you know, we're all
confused, we're just looking

13:25.674 --> 13:28.938
at each other, and Charlie goes,
"What do you mean, sir?"

13:28.982 --> 13:33.203
And Washington goes,
"Goofy's a dog,

13:33.203 --> 13:36.990
"and Pluto's a dog,
so why is he letting Mickey

13:37.033 --> 13:39.122
do that to one of his own?"

13:40.863 --> 13:44.127
Yeah, T had them
running scared from day one.

13:44.127 --> 13:48.566
I was, uh, fresh-faced
and wild-eyed.

13:48.653 --> 13:53.441
I was an inbetweener
for about three years,

13:53.484 --> 13:55.051
didn't see much movement.

13:57.358 --> 14:00.752
An inbetweener was usually
for animators just starting ou.

14:00.796 --> 14:04.887
So, say a character
slaps somebody across the face.

14:04.931 --> 14:08.456
They would get a senior artist
to do the fun part,

14:08.499 --> 14:13.896
drawing the first and the last
frame that define the movement.

14:13.940 --> 14:18.292
The inbetweener draws all the
many little frames in between.

14:19.815 --> 14:21.730
I was stuck as an inbetweener

14:21.730 --> 14:23.819
for years and years,

14:23.819 --> 14:25.952
but as soon as Thomas came in,
he said,

14:26.039 --> 14:28.606
"I want you to be
lead director on this."

14:29.912 --> 14:33.350
And I said, "On what?"
And he said,

14:33.394 --> 14:36.092
"The Blackest movie
of all time."

14:37.833 --> 14:39.835
-♪ Check, check, uh♪
-♪ Get it, get, get...♪

14:39.922 --> 14:43.970
He said he wanted to make
a movie about Black fatherhood.

14:43.970 --> 14:46.233
♪ Get, get, get it...♪

14:46.276 --> 14:48.713
Man, he wanted to tackle
everything in this movie.

14:48.757 --> 14:52.630
Segregation, single parenting,

14:52.630 --> 14:54.850
low-income career trajectories,

14:54.937 --> 14:58.419
fear of gang violence, incarceration,

14:58.419 --> 15:02.640
the amount of cheese
in African-American diets.

15:02.640 --> 15:07.297
He wanted to make a movie that
covered all this and more...

15:07.384 --> 15:10.431
and he said he knew
the perfect character to do it.

15:10.431 --> 15:12.389
♪ Swingin' while I'm singin'♪

15:12.433 --> 15:13.869
♪ Hey!♪

15:13.869 --> 15:18.221
Thomas, man,
he knew that people thought

15:18.308 --> 15:21.094
Goofy was dumb,
but he wanted to show

15:21.094 --> 15:24.097
the systemic factors
that Goofy was dealing with.

15:24.140 --> 15:27.404
A shitty job, angry kid,

15:27.448 --> 15:30.233
and embarrassed
by his lack of influence.

15:31.539 --> 15:36.457
Man, it was wild how deep
he leaned in to all this,

15:36.544 --> 15:38.676
'cause, uh, as far as I knew,

15:38.763 --> 15:42.463
Thomas had a really solid homelife.

15:42.463 --> 15:45.466
It's a vacation with me
and my best buddy.

15:45.466 --> 15:46.554
Oh, Donald Duck?

15:46.597 --> 15:49.209
No, silly, with you!

16:02.570 --> 16:05.834
Thomas and I,
we were married young.

16:05.921 --> 16:09.011
We didn't have much,

16:09.055 --> 16:12.232
and, um, you know what, he nevr

16:12.275 --> 16:16.018
let that get
in the way of us, though.

16:16.062 --> 16:17.454
If there was something
we couldn't afford,

16:17.498 --> 16:19.587
he would just draw it.

16:19.630 --> 16:22.938
You know,
you'd wake up and I would

16:22.938 --> 16:27.725
see anything from a coffee makr
to a solid-gold jet

16:27.725 --> 16:31.294
on the refrigerator
in the morning.

16:31.294 --> 16:32.774
That was Thomas.

16:34.167 --> 16:38.040
We-we had our son Maxwell
pretty early.

16:38.084 --> 16:40.086
He and Thomas,
they were inseparable.

16:40.173 --> 16:42.175
He did everything for that boy.

16:42.218 --> 16:45.265
Oh, my God, look at this...

16:45.308 --> 16:46.962
Oh, look at this!

16:47.006 --> 16:50.096
Thomas kept saying that

16:50.183 --> 16:52.315
A Goofy Movie was for him.

16:53.664 --> 16:58.408
He'd say, "If I'm not here,
you can show him this."

16:58.452 --> 17:02.630
I was too scared to ask him
what he meant by that.

17:02.630 --> 17:05.850
To give you a little context,
when A Goofy Movie came out,

17:05.894 --> 17:09.376
Black masculinity in the media
was in a really weird place.

17:09.419 --> 17:13.554
- Hello, I'm Blaine Edwards.
- And I'm Antoine Merriweather!

17:13.641 --> 17:15.991
And welcome to Men on Films.

17:16.078 --> 17:17.775
The show that looks at movies..

17:17.862 --> 17:20.213
...from a male point of view.

17:20.300 --> 17:23.216
So on one hand,
you've got men doing, kind of

17:23.303 --> 17:26.306
"faux queer" comedy,
and on the other end

17:26.349 --> 17:29.135
of the spectrum, you've got
these hypermasculine portrayals.

17:31.833 --> 17:34.575
Am I my brother's keeper?

17:36.707 --> 17:38.187
Yes, I am.

17:40.798 --> 17:43.366
And, by contrast, Goofy

17:43.453 --> 17:46.587
is a nuanced portrayal
of a Black man

17:46.587 --> 17:49.677
whose priority is his family.

17:49.720 --> 17:52.810
- What?
- Nothin'.

17:52.810 --> 17:56.118
Goofy really put forward
the philosophy that...

17:56.162 --> 18:01.254
just love your children for
who they are and accept them,

18:01.254 --> 18:03.038
and deal with the pain
that the world

18:03.038 --> 18:04.170
is not going to love them
like you do.

18:12.961 --> 18:17.052
I think in the movie,
Dad laid in a lot of things.

18:17.052 --> 18:20.360
I-I notice it more now
than I did then.

18:20.403 --> 18:22.057
♪ If I could make you
stop and take a look♪

18:22.144 --> 18:23.580
♪ At me instead of just...♪

18:23.624 --> 18:28.019
In the beginning,
Max sings a song,

18:28.063 --> 18:31.458
dunks a basketball, and then
is cheered on by his peers.

18:33.286 --> 18:35.288
I think my dad
was making a comment

18:35.331 --> 18:36.854
on Black exceptionalism

18:36.854 --> 18:40.075
and how some people believe

18:40.075 --> 18:44.732
that performative assimilation
is the best way to get by.

18:44.819 --> 18:49.171
My dad wanted to prove those
limitations were just that,

18:49.215 --> 18:52.609
limits put on us by outsiders.

18:52.653 --> 18:56.744
That's why he made it
a fishing trip.

18:56.744 --> 18:59.138
It's the same one
we used to take. [chuckles]

19:01.183 --> 19:03.446
The trip was
supposed to start out

19:03.446 --> 19:06.493
as sort of like a freedom ride.

19:06.536 --> 19:08.930
And the map,
which is central to the plot

19:08.973 --> 19:13.674
of the film and also representd
Goofy's trust in his son Max,

19:13.674 --> 19:16.285
was supposed to
remind the viewer

19:16.329 --> 19:18.983
of the Green Book
and how Black travelers

19:19.027 --> 19:21.508
had to use it
to advance their way

19:21.551 --> 19:25.555
through the Jim Crow South
during the '30s and the '40s.

19:25.599 --> 19:29.559
A lot of the film had
deeper meanings, and word

19:29.646 --> 19:34.434
got around that it meant
a lot more in Thomas's eyes.

19:34.477 --> 19:37.872
Dad wanted every Black
person in America to feel like

19:37.915 --> 19:42.572
they were just a road trip away
from being part of a movement.

19:42.616 --> 19:46.359
Even if their kids
didn't understand the stakes.

19:46.446 --> 19:50.363
It was about destiny
and how our children,

19:50.406 --> 19:53.366
our future,
get to draw our path.

19:53.453 --> 19:55.368
I want to get us
to doing something

19:55.411 --> 19:56.717
we've never done before.

19:56.804 --> 19:58.806
We have never truly drawn

19:58.893 --> 20:02.636
authentically Black characters,
characters that act...

20:08.163 --> 20:10.731
Now, see,
this is what I'm talking about.

20:10.818 --> 20:13.734
This is what I'm talking about.

20:13.821 --> 20:17.172
In order to draw
authentically Black characters,

20:17.259 --> 20:19.305
one has to know Black people.

20:19.392 --> 20:23.831
Okay? Now,
Tom went out on assignment.

20:23.874 --> 20:27.617
Researched, hung out,
went to a couple Black cookouts.

20:27.661 --> 20:31.969
Said the wrong thing,
got his ass beat, but now, now?

20:31.969 --> 20:34.537
He's more aware,
he's more knowledgeable

20:34.624 --> 20:36.496
of how to draw. Okay?

20:36.539 --> 20:39.716
Thomas tried to pull back
and be more

20:39.760 --> 20:43.416
subtle with his message,
uh, when the complaints started,

20:43.416 --> 20:45.766
and we were getting complaints
from everywhere.

20:45.766 --> 20:51.206
But there was
nothing subtle about Thomas.

20:51.206 --> 20:53.904
At one point, he said he
didn't want Mickey in the movie.

20:53.904 --> 20:56.429
And, um, I kept saying,

20:56.429 --> 20:58.779
"But it's Disney.
What are you talking about?"

20:58.779 --> 21:01.869
I can remember
Thomas ranting in his office,

21:01.869 --> 21:04.175
"They're trying to make me
put this white boy in my movie."

21:04.219 --> 21:07.788
And I'm thinking,
"He's a mouse."

21:07.875 --> 21:10.094
He told me from the beginning,

21:10.138 --> 21:13.010
"I'm not here for a long time,
I'm here for a good time."

21:13.097 --> 21:16.449
Uh, he knew
he was going to get fired,

21:16.536 --> 21:19.365
but the "when" of it
was fun for him.

21:19.452 --> 21:21.236
It was like a game.

21:21.323 --> 21:26.894
We had 15, 16,
sometimes 18-hour days.

21:26.894 --> 21:30.593
And, uh, he would be screaming,
"We don't dance like that."

21:30.593 --> 21:34.380
And he would
bring his friend's kids in

21:34.467 --> 21:37.470
and, uh,
they would moonwalk and,

21:37.470 --> 21:40.777
and, you know, do all, like,
you know, this stuff, you know?

21:40.821 --> 21:42.823
Oh, man,
I must have drawn a pair

21:42.910 --> 21:46.479
of gloves dapping,
I don't know, over 5,000 times?

21:46.479 --> 21:49.003
I just kept hearing, "That's
not a dap, that's not a dap.

21:49.046 --> 21:50.134
Where's the snap?"

21:50.178 --> 21:51.571
You see my knees?

21:51.614 --> 21:54.443
You see this,
see this angle of these knees?

21:54.487 --> 21:57.054
Draw-- Draw this!
Get this down, okay?

21:57.141 --> 21:59.230
One time,
I just couldn't take it--

21:59.274 --> 22:02.103
my fingers were just bleeding--
so I went up to talk to him,

22:02.146 --> 22:04.497
and he was up there drinking
whiskey with Robert Townsend,

22:04.584 --> 22:08.501
and Janet Jackson, uh,
Sinbad and Adina Howard.

22:08.588 --> 22:11.721
Well, when I poked
my head in, Thomas went,

22:11.765 --> 22:13.288
"Who ordered the white rice?"

22:13.332 --> 22:15.638
And they all just laughed,

22:15.725 --> 22:19.076
and, uh, I just didn't get it.

22:19.076 --> 22:20.817
BRIAN McKNIGHT:
Everybody used to be up there.

22:20.861 --> 22:24.734
Eddie Murphy,
Arsenio Hall, Kadeem Hardison,

22:24.778 --> 22:28.738
Harrison Ford, it was crazy.
The parties were insane.

22:28.782 --> 22:31.959
Imagine Disney after 
The Lion King.

22:32.002 --> 22:34.701
You got this smooth-looking
brother at the helm.

22:38.661 --> 22:40.968
It was crazy
he even had that office.

22:41.011 --> 22:42.622
Everyone would go
up there and just hang

22:42.665 --> 22:45.625
as we plotted our overthrow
of Hollywood.

22:45.668 --> 22:47.453
You couldn't tell us nothing.

22:47.453 --> 22:49.890
They had a big
musical number planned

22:49.890 --> 22:52.196
with Bryan Adams in the movie.

22:52.240 --> 22:55.461
And one day,
Thomas asked me to come by

22:55.461 --> 22:57.898
and see what he's working on.

22:57.898 --> 23:00.074
And we're walking through,
and he stops in one

23:00.117 --> 23:01.902
of the rooms and plays me
some of the music.

23:01.989 --> 23:05.122
And I'm like,
"Yeah, you know, pop ballad.

23:05.209 --> 23:06.646
"You know, same old, same old.

23:06.689 --> 23:09.518
You know, it's-it's good,
it's-it's cool."

23:09.562 --> 23:12.869
But he asks me,
"Who do you like right now?"

23:12.913 --> 23:15.045
I'm like, "You know,
I-I've been listening

23:15.132 --> 23:17.221
to this Tevin Campbell."

23:17.265 --> 23:21.704
And from then on, it just
had to be Tevin Campbell.

23:21.791 --> 23:26.709
♪ Last night I, I saw you♪

23:26.709 --> 23:28.058
♪ Standing♪

23:28.058 --> 23:30.365
♪ And I started♪

23:30.409 --> 23:32.715
♪ Started pretending♪

23:32.715 --> 23:34.500
Most of the cats
around there didn't

23:34.587 --> 23:36.371
even know who
Tevin Campbell was.

23:36.458 --> 23:40.810
For three months, a lot of them
were saying Kevin Campbell.

23:40.810 --> 23:45.728
♪ Can we talk, baby,
can we talk for a minute?♪

23:45.815 --> 23:47.948
♪ Girl, I want
to know your name♪

23:47.948 --> 23:50.341
♪ I want to know your name♪

23:50.385 --> 23:51.952
Thomas was a cool cat.

23:52.039 --> 23:54.824
But the brother definitely had
a chip on his shoulder.

23:54.824 --> 23:56.435
He didn't like it if
you challenged his Blackness.

23:59.612 --> 24:01.701
I think there was
a part of Thomas

24:01.744 --> 24:05.531
that kind of felt that
being an animator

24:05.531 --> 24:08.534
wasn't innately Black, you know?

24:08.534 --> 24:11.537
And he wanted to make it Black,

24:11.537 --> 24:13.277
he wanted to make it cool.

24:13.321 --> 24:17.368
He wanted to-to carve out
his own space and not feel

24:17.412 --> 24:20.981
that he was divorced
from his own culture, you know.

24:21.068 --> 24:24.506
He was loading his culture
into this stuff that

24:24.550 --> 24:26.987
we were doing so that
it just seemed normal

24:27.074 --> 24:30.817
and would seem normal
to the rest of us.

24:30.860 --> 24:33.994
He had a lot of pressure, man--
the culture, the job itself,

24:33.994 --> 24:36.866
what-what he was trying to live
up to that I'm not even sure

24:36.866 --> 24:38.781
that he knew that he could
live up to it.

24:38.781 --> 24:42.742
And, uh, it had to
take a huge toll on him.

24:42.785 --> 24:44.961
One morning I walk in
the studio, I got my coffee,

24:45.005 --> 24:47.398
I'm ready to sit down,
get to busi-- business.

24:47.442 --> 24:50.750
And Thomas like, "Psst! Psst!
Come here, come here!"

24:50.793 --> 24:53.666
So I go over there and, uh,
he's in his cubicle,

24:53.709 --> 24:57.234
he got this briefcase,
he opens it up,

24:57.278 --> 24:58.801
there's a gun inside!

24:58.801 --> 25:03.240
I'm like,
"Dude, what-what is this?

25:03.284 --> 25:05.112
What's it for?"

25:05.155 --> 25:09.682
And he's like,
"This is to remind me that

25:09.682 --> 25:11.945
they don't know
who the fuck I am."

25:17.690 --> 25:21.128
Dad kind of started to lose it.

25:21.171 --> 25:24.044
He would stay up all night,
talk to hisself.

25:24.131 --> 25:28.309
After a while, Mom...
she had had enough.

25:30.920 --> 25:33.488
After a while,
Mom couldn't take it anymore.

25:33.488 --> 25:35.272
He had just gotten too mean.

25:37.187 --> 25:39.712
We had so much fun.

25:40.626 --> 25:44.717
But then, it wasn't fun anymor.

25:44.804 --> 25:47.284
He had been
fooling around, yeah.

25:47.371 --> 25:51.288
And drinking a lot,
and he got pretty mean.

25:51.375 --> 25:54.291
He would say some mean,
nasty things to me,

25:54.378 --> 25:57.512
which he never,
he had never done that before.

26:00.036 --> 26:02.038
I remember at one time,
Maxwell tried to step in

26:02.082 --> 26:03.300
between his dad and I,

26:03.344 --> 26:06.303
and Thomas...

26:06.390 --> 26:10.090
was not kind.
He kind of...

26:12.309 --> 26:15.182
He kind of raised his, um...

26:15.225 --> 26:18.228
he raised his fist...

26:18.315 --> 26:20.883
at Maxwell.

26:20.970 --> 26:24.191
That wasn't cool,
because they-they

26:24.234 --> 26:25.801
had always been so close.

26:28.804 --> 26:30.676
He was a...

26:30.763 --> 26:33.113
he was a really good father.

26:33.200 --> 26:36.116
I mean, at least he tried
to be a good father.

26:36.116 --> 26:39.075
I kept saying, "Thomas,

26:39.119 --> 26:41.904
"you're going to lose him,

26:41.904 --> 26:44.820
you're going to lose your son."

26:47.344 --> 26:50.913
But all he kept saying is...

26:50.913 --> 26:53.046
"But this is for him."

26:55.918 --> 26:58.660
I knew things
were going downhill when

26:58.704 --> 27:00.793
Thomas's wife left him.

27:00.880 --> 27:03.056
Sh-She had been there
from the beginning.

27:04.361 --> 27:06.929
And he started
sleeping in his office.

27:07.016 --> 27:11.020
And, um, one day,
there was a board meeting.

27:11.107 --> 27:15.764
All the Disney heads were ther,
and they started talking

27:15.808 --> 27:19.550
about the budget was
going a bit high on Goofy.

27:19.594 --> 27:22.684
And one of the guys
asked Thomas, he said, uh,

27:22.728 --> 27:24.904
"Are you in control
of the budget?"

27:24.947 --> 27:26.819
And Thomas said,
"Of course I am.

27:26.862 --> 27:28.908
I'm Goofy."

27:28.951 --> 27:30.692
And then,

27:30.736 --> 27:35.479
he did this this chilling laug. Like...

27:39.092 --> 27:42.617
You know, l-like Goofy,
but broken.

27:43.966 --> 27:46.534
:
And it-it was terrifying, man.

27:46.534 --> 27:48.101
I almost started crying.

27:50.407 --> 27:52.758
He really thought he was Goofy.

27:58.981 --> 28:01.070
The company knew he had to go,

28:01.114 --> 28:04.857
but legally,
he was still the CEO.

28:04.900 --> 28:08.556
That gentleman's
show of hands was, uh,

28:08.643 --> 28:12.647
surprisingly hard to go back o.

28:12.734 --> 28:15.084
You know, they even tried to
buy him out of his contract.

28:15.128 --> 28:18.784
Yeah, they offered him,
like $75,000.

28:18.871 --> 28:20.786
No, no.

28:20.873 --> 28:24.615
We offered him $75 million,

28:24.659 --> 28:27.096
paid out over ten years.

28:27.183 --> 28:32.014
Um, that's how... that's how
serious it was for us.

28:32.101 --> 28:33.842
I mean, he didn't take it.

28:33.886 --> 28:37.585
But, uh, maybe he
should have taken it, you know?

28:39.892 --> 28:43.460
He started
getting really paranoid.

28:43.547 --> 28:46.986
He'd say he was
afraid for his life,

28:47.029 --> 28:49.118
but he'd never say from whom.

28:50.424 --> 28:52.687
Suddenly, there were
a bunch of guys in bow ties

28:52.774 --> 28:54.776
hanging around the office.

28:54.820 --> 28:56.691
He introduced us
to one of them saying,

28:56.778 --> 28:59.041
"I want you to meet my new
head of security,

28:59.041 --> 29:02.044
Al Shaheim X."

29:02.044 --> 29:04.699
The thing not a lot of people
knew about,

29:04.699 --> 29:07.267
was Thomas had
been going around town

29:07.354 --> 29:12.141
letting a lot of people know
how huge this was going to be,

29:12.141 --> 29:15.492
and he had a lot of different
Black empowerment groups,

29:15.492 --> 29:20.715
radical movements, and just
straight-up gangs all wanting

29:20.802 --> 29:25.154
as much influence and subtle
shout-outs as the other.

29:25.198 --> 29:27.940
You know,
I think that he had made

29:27.983 --> 29:29.985
way too many deals at one poin.

29:31.291 --> 29:34.250
At least, that's what...
that's what I heard.

29:34.294 --> 29:40.300
Truthfully, it all came down
to the end of the movie.

29:40.343 --> 29:42.345
Really.

29:45.000 --> 29:47.698
Oh, man, the end of the movie.

29:47.742 --> 29:49.657
I heard he wanted Goofy
and Max to get pulled over

29:49.744 --> 29:52.486
early on and, uh, you know...

29:56.751 --> 29:59.885
Wh-Why would he do that?
That makes no sense.

30:01.190 --> 30:04.411
That Powerline concert
at the end,

30:04.411 --> 30:07.196
it wasn't supposed to be
all hunky-dory, dancing-dancing.

30:07.240 --> 30:09.677
Nah, it wasn't supposed
to be like that at all.

30:12.288 --> 30:15.204
I heard Goofy was supposed
to get shot at the concert.

30:15.248 --> 30:17.990
You know,
a Black man runs onstage

30:18.033 --> 30:20.557
like that at a major venue?
Come on, man.

30:20.644 --> 30:22.646
There's something wrong here.

30:22.690 --> 30:24.561
Thomas was trying
to make a statement.

30:26.302 --> 30:29.262
Do you, uh,
think his death was an accident?

30:31.133 --> 30:32.787
Next question, man.

30:32.831 --> 30:34.745
No, I don't.

30:34.789 --> 30:37.009
I-I've never thought
it was an accident.

30:38.445 --> 30:40.316
Why not?

30:40.360 --> 30:42.797
Um, well...

30:44.146 --> 30:46.235
...partly because of
his-his mood at the end,

30:46.279 --> 30:48.934
but, um, you know,
there was also the tape.

30:50.500 --> 30:51.806
What tape?

31:00.684 --> 31:03.949
♪ For the first time ever♪

31:04.036 --> 31:06.952
♪ Seeing it eye to eye♪

31:08.605 --> 31:13.610
Oh, I'm so sorry.

31:13.610 --> 31:15.612
Oh, man, I...

31:15.699 --> 31:21.488
Ooh, I'm close, I'm-I'm close...

31:21.531 --> 31:23.664
I'm so close.

31:37.199 --> 31:39.419
I got to finish this.

31:44.859 --> 31:47.122
:
I got to finish it.

31:50.299 --> 31:54.086
Back then, mental health wasn't
something that we talked about.

31:54.173 --> 31:56.653
I didn't even know
what to characterize

31:56.653 --> 32:00.179
what I saw him going through,
what it... what it was.

32:00.222 --> 32:03.051
I just knew he was going
through something, um,

32:03.095 --> 32:06.011
something that was very, very
difficult for him to deal with.

32:08.230 --> 32:10.885
This is a little hard
for me right now.

32:13.801 --> 32:16.543
I-I don't know why.

32:16.630 --> 32:21.330
When the news finally broke
that Dad was officially out,

32:21.330 --> 32:24.681
they let him back on the lot
for one final screening

32:24.768 --> 32:27.032
of what they were able to
piece together behind his back.

32:29.991 --> 32:31.123
He didn't know they
had been working

32:31.166 --> 32:32.689
on the movie without him...

32:32.776 --> 32:35.040
changing and corrupting
his original vision.

32:36.171 --> 32:39.566
He-he didn't take the news well.

32:39.609 --> 32:41.568
I was at that last screening.

32:41.655 --> 32:44.353
It was the Bigfoot stuff
that put him over the edge.

32:44.440 --> 32:46.921
They'd added it
without his knowledge.

32:46.921 --> 32:48.923
He'd wanted Goofy and Max

32:48.923 --> 32:51.273
to wander into a thrift store

32:51.360 --> 32:54.320
and discover
Huey Newton's rattan throne.

32:54.363 --> 32:57.671
And once they sat in it,
Max would finally understand

32:57.714 --> 32:59.238
what Goofy had been fighting

32:59.281 --> 33:01.718
to make him understand
all along.

33:01.718 --> 33:06.593
Instead, they took out
the thrift store and the throne

33:06.636 --> 33:09.726
and put in that Bigfoot bit.

33:09.726 --> 33:12.686
It's like they were saying,
"What you were after,

33:12.729 --> 33:16.298
"your message,
it's a fantasy as elusive

33:16.298 --> 33:18.213
and unreal as Bigfoot."

33:19.519 --> 33:21.042
You're going to see...

33:21.086 --> 33:23.001
you're going to be
proud of me...

33:24.872 --> 33:27.266
I'm doing this for you.

33:27.309 --> 33:29.746
I'm doing this for all of us.

33:32.749 --> 33:34.403
Oh, man.

33:36.449 --> 33:40.540
I could feel him
get up and leave

33:40.627 --> 33:43.412
and I-I heard the door close
and I went out after him,

33:43.456 --> 33:46.546
and by the time I got
to the parking lot, he was gone.

33:46.546 --> 33:49.549
Something... I don't know,
there was a look in his eye.

33:49.549 --> 33:52.552
He-he just got in the car
and he took off...

33:53.857 --> 33:55.163
...and didn't come back.

34:16.010 --> 34:18.143
They, um...

34:18.230 --> 34:20.493
they never found his body.

34:26.455 --> 34:31.286
I think that was...
especially hard for Mom.

34:34.594 --> 34:35.725
Dad!

34:39.686 --> 34:42.036
We actually watch...

34:42.080 --> 34:43.603
we watch the movies a lot.

34:43.690 --> 34:46.910
Uh, I couldn't for a while.

34:46.954 --> 34:52.046
But... but once I did,
I couldn't stop.

34:56.181 --> 34:57.965
It's like, um,

34:57.965 --> 35:00.402
it was like having him around,

35:00.489 --> 35:03.275
you know, in a fun way.

35:03.318 --> 35:04.667
Yeah.

35:14.286 --> 35:18.333
You know that, uh, "Damn,
you live like this?" meme?

35:19.639 --> 35:23.164
It's so funny to me
because the essence of it

35:23.208 --> 35:26.167
is those drawings
he did in college.

35:26.211 --> 35:30.171
Black folks living
their lives, being funny,

35:30.215 --> 35:34.654
being free, being real.

35:38.875 --> 35:41.443
He would've
been really proud that

35:41.530 --> 35:43.228
he became part of the culture.

35:44.446 --> 35:46.187
I think that's
what he wanted in the end,

35:46.231 --> 35:48.450
to just be part of the family.

35:51.366 --> 35:53.107
And along the way...

35:54.543 --> 35:57.546
...he made the Blackest
movie of all time.

36:04.727 --> 36:07.121
♪ Yeah, yeah♪

36:12.779 --> 36:15.564
♪ Got myself a notion♪

36:15.608 --> 36:20.482
♪ One I know
that you'll understand♪

36:20.569 --> 36:23.181
♪ To set the world in motion♪

36:24.486 --> 36:28.011
♪ By reaching out
for each other's hands♪

36:28.055 --> 36:32.625
♪ Maybe we'll discover What we
should have known all along♪

36:32.625 --> 36:35.715
♪ Yeah♪

36:35.802 --> 36:37.151
♪ One way or another♪

36:37.238 --> 36:40.372
♪ Together's
where we both belong♪

36:40.459 --> 36:43.723
♪ If we listen to
each other's heart♪

